  <article class="note prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-a:text-blue-700 prose-a:underline">
    <p class="kicker">A second note from the founder</p>
    <h1>Why This Library Takes No Side</h1>
    <p class="org">America's School Trust Library</p>

    <p class="editor-note">
      <em>
        Editor's note. Dave Sullivan wrote this note in June 2026, before the
        Library had a board; it is published here as written, with its original
        signature. The founding board it anticipates was not seated until
        August 3, 2026, and its membership has changed since. For the directors
        serving now, see <a href="/about">About the Library</a>.
      </em>
    </p>

    <p class="lead">
      There is already <a href="/about/founder/">a note from me on this site</a>
      that explains who I am and why I built this Library. This is a second
      note, about a harder question. The first one asks whether you can trust
      me. This one asks something I find more important: whether you can trust
      the Library after I am gone, when good intentions are no longer enough to
      hold it together.
    </p>

    <p>
      Start with the trouble the Library is meant to answer. The lands at the
      center of this story were set aside, beginning in 1785, to support the
      education of children — held in trust, the way a guardian holds money for
      a minor. But look at who manages them today. In most states the agency is
      called a Department of State Lands, not a Department of School Lands. That
      is not a small slip of naming. Over decades, an institution comes to serve
      the people who actually show up — its own staff, its own budget, the
      constituents who walk through the door and complain. The schoolchildren do
      not walk through the door. The children not yet born cannot. A trust meant
      to last forever is hard to keep precisely because the future has no vote,
      and the present always does.
    </p>

    <p>
      So the honest reader should turn that same suspicion on me. If I build a
      fact-keeping institution and ask the country to rely on it for two hundred
      years, what stops it from drifting the same way — from quietly keeping the
      facts that flatter its friends and misplacing the ones that don't? That is
      exactly the right question to ask of anything that calls itself neutral. I
      would rather answer it plainly than ask you to take my word.
    </p>

    <p>
      My answer comes from my father. He flew thirty missions over Europe as a
      B-17 co-pilot in the war, which is reason enough for me to call him my
      hero, and by the arithmetic of that job reason enough that I might never
      have been born. He came home and went to work at the Oregon Statesman,
      first as a reporter and in time as the editor of the whole paper. From him
      I learned a distinction he treated as sacred. A reporter records what
      happened — who, what, when, where, why — and leaves the verdict to the
      reader. The editorial page, kept physically and institutionally separate,
      is where the paper says what it believes ought to happen. The newsroom
      looks at the past and describes it. The editorial page looks at the future
      and argues about it. My father knew the wall between them was hard to hold
      — even the choice of what to cover is a kind of judgment, and a reporter
      who skips the rally he dislikes has already failed at the job. But the
      difficulty was the reason for the discipline, not an excuse to abandon it.
    </p>

    <p>
      That distinction is the whole design of what we are building. There are
      two kinds of organizations in this work, and they must not be the same
      thing. The advocacy groups — Oregon Advocates for School Trust Lands
      (OASTL), the Oregon group I lead, and Advocates for School Trust Lands
      (ASTL), the broader effort alongside it — are the editorial page. They
      take positions. They sue states they believe are breaking faith with the
      trust. They propose model laws. That is their job, and it is honorable
      work. The Library is the front page. It records what happened — the ruling
      a judge handed down in Alaska, the statute a legislature passed, the
      transaction an agency approved — and it takes no side on whether any of it
      was right or wrong. When someone proposes a model law, the Library does
      not endorse it. It holds the record and reports, evenhandedly, here is
      what these people are arguing for, and here is what those people argue
      back. Then it gets out of the way and lets you decide.
    </p>

    <p>
      I want to be precise about what keeps that wall standing, because a wall
      made only of good intentions is the wall that has failed at more than one
      great newspaper. The Library's wall is built into how it works, not just
      into what it promises. It keeps the record whether or not the record helps
      its friends. It cites its sources so that any reader can go check the
      original for themselves. It keeps a public correction practice and openly
      invites you to catch its mistakes. The discipline underneath all of that
      is completeness: a library that tries to hold everything about its subject
      can be caught leaving something out, in a way that an opinion never can.
      That is the real safeguard — not that we promise to be fair, but that our
      work is laid open to be audited against the full record by anyone who
      doubts us. Neutrality you have to take on faith is worth nothing.
      Neutrality you can check is worth everything.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now the disclosure I owe you, because my own situation is the sharpest
      test of every word above. I am the president of OASTL, an advocacy group.
      I am also a founding director of this Library. By my father's rule those
      are opposite jobs, and you are right to wonder whether one person can hold
      both without the advocate leaking into the librarian. For now my answer is
      partly practical and, I hope, honest: the only people ready to stand up a
      Library like this on its first day are the people who already cared enough
      to join the fight — so the founding board is drawn largely from OASTL and
      ASTL, and every one of us who serves the Library commits to act, in that
      room, as a reporter and not an advocate. Everyone is entitled to an
      opinion. No one is entitled to their own facts.
    </p>

    <p>
      But a starting condition is not a resting place, and I will not pretend it
      is. Within about the first year, the Library's board separates from shared
      membership with the advocacy groups, so that no single person sits in
      control of both the side that argues and the side that records. For me, I
      expect that will mean a real choice: to help guide the advocacy, or to
      help keep the Library, but not to run both. I would rather set that plan
      down here, in public, while it is still a promise you can hold me to, than
      discover the conflict years from now when it has grown roots and is harder
      to pull out.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is a reason this matters beyond one library and one set of lands.
      The country is about to build new forever-institutions — for the age of
      artificial intelligence now arriving — and every one of them will face
      this same question of how a thing meant to last keeps faith with people
      who cannot yet speak for themselves. A library that can prove it keeps the
      whole record, the inconvenient parts included, is one small working model
      of how that is done. And if you ever find this Library keeping only the
      convenient facts, you will have caught us failing at the one thing we
      exist for. Tell us where we went wrong, and don't pull any punches.
    </p>

    <p class="sig">
      — <strong>Dave Sullivan</strong><br />
      Founder and President, America's School Trust Library<br />
      President, Oregon Advocates for School Trust Lands<br />
      Oregon, June 2026
    </p>

